                    %a{:href => "#on_minkowski_spacetime_2"} On Minkowski spacetime
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            %strong scalar field
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            on
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            which is simply a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            with values in the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+of+scalars"} field of scalars
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternions"} quaternions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℍ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{H}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+bundle"} field bundle
            (of which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            is a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/section"} section
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+line+bundle"} trivial line bundle
          %p
            One fundamental (complex, charged) scalar field is seen in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experiment
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inflaton"} inflaton
            field in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28physics%29"} models
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
          %p
            But scalar fields also serve as a key toy example in theoretical studies of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"} phi^4 theory
            or in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ising+model"} Ising model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical field theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            is due to it already exhibiting much of the core structure of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+theory"} field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            of general
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theories"} local field theories
            are structurally those of the scalar field, just with some more fairly evident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theoretic
            structure thrown in.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#free_scalar_field Free scalar field
          %p
            We discuss here the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field"} scalar field
            on general
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background field
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
            with itself. This means that its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+Lagrangian+density"} local Lagrangian density
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadratic+form"} quadratic
            in the fields and its first derivatives (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LocalLagrangianOfFreeScalarField"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+equation"} Klein-Gordon equation
            (hence the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+equation"} wave equation
            in the case of vanishing
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass"} mass
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeScalarFieldEOM"}
            below).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of this system (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FreeScalarFieldEOM"}
            below) turns out to have as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
            (i.e. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+function"} Green function
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support+of+a+distribution"} support
            is inside the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/light+cone"} light cone
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+functions"} Green functions
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein-Gordon+operator"} Klein-Gordon operator
            (the “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"}> propagators
            ”, such as the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            of the scalar field (see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S-matrix"} S-matrix
            for more).
          %h4#covariant_phase_space Covariant phase space
          %p
            Recall that a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+field+theory"} local field theory
            is for some prescribed class of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifolds"} manifolds
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            of given
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
